WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.760
Does a window close on its own, like a clock?

2
00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:08.200
Or does experience help close it?

3
00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:14.720
A young male zebra finch memorizes his tutor, usually his father, between about day

4
00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:16.220
25 and 65.

5
00:00:17.333 --> 00:00:21.373
He settles on a fixed, or crystallized, song by about three months.

6
00:00:22.383 --> 00:00:23.783
Now take two groups.

7
00:00:23.783 --> 00:00:25.843
One hears its father sing.

8
00:00:25.843 --> 00:00:28.723
The other is raised without adult males.

9
00:00:29.767 --> 00:00:32.107
Both get a new tutor at the same late age.

10
00:00:32.987 --> 00:00:36.067
By then, normally reared males have stopped copying new song.

11
00:00:37.067 --> 00:00:40.187
Predict. Which group copies the new tutor?

12
00:00:41.087 --> 00:00:42.567
What would a clock set by age predict?

13
00:00:49.017 --> 00:00:50.797
A clock predicts neither copies.

14
00:00:51.717 --> 00:00:56.037
But Lucy Eales reported that only the birds raised without adult males copied.

15
00:00:57.067 --> 00:01:01.407
That was in 1985. Later studies gave mixed results.

16
00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:04.150
Same age, different history.

17
00:01:04.990 --> 00:01:06.750
So age alone may not set the window.

18
00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:09.530
Hearing a tutor seems to help close it.

19
00:01:10.433 --> 00:01:14.153
But missing input delays the closing; it does not abolish it.

20
00:01:14.893 --> 00:01:17.613
These birds eventually crystallize a song of their own.

21
00:01:21.467 --> 00:01:23.707
So experience seems to help set the window.

22
00:01:24.727 --> 00:01:26.487
Now, which part of the brain does the learning?

23
00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:33.490
The motor pathway runs from HVC to RA, to brainstem neurons that drive the vocal

24
00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:34.490
organ.

25
00:01:34.883 --> 00:01:36.563
A second loop leaves HVC.

26
00:01:37.523 --> 00:01:41.283
It runs through area X and the thalamus to LMAN, then back to RA.

27
00:01:42.483 --> 00:01:47.823
LMAN lesions disrupted song development in young finches, but left stable adult song

28
00:01:47.823 --> 00:01:50.923
intact. This loop matters while song is learned.

29
00:01:51.933 --> 00:01:55.393
In this loop, NMDA receptors were a natural suspect.

30
00:01:56.453 --> 00:01:59.773
Glutamate and a helper molecule, a co-agonist, activate them.

31
00:02:00.733 --> 00:02:06.153
But at normal magnesium levels, magnesium plugs the pore until the receiving cell is

32
00:02:06.153 --> 00:02:07.153
depolarized.

33
00:02:08.967 --> 00:02:13.567
So current flows mainly when glutamate is released and the receiving cell is

34
00:02:13.567 --> 00:02:15.707
depolarized at about the same time.

35
00:02:16.567 --> 00:02:19.627
In many brain regions, that strengthens the active synapse.

36
00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:24.000
Mark Basham and colleagues infused a blocker, AP5.

37
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:27.900
Predict. Which birds copy poorly?

38
00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:33.580
Blocker in the anterior forebrain before tutoring, there on other days only, or in

39
00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:34.580
the cerebellum?

40
00:02:40.633 --> 00:02:42.493
Only the first group copied less.

41
00:02:43.483 --> 00:02:46.403
Birds given AP5 on other days copied normally.

42
00:02:47.223 --> 00:02:51.183
That counts against lost practice alone, though birds could catch up between

43
00:02:51.183 --> 00:02:52.183
infusions.

44
00:02:52.783 --> 00:02:55.423
Cerebellum infusions left learning normal.

45
00:02:56.323 --> 00:03:01.043
And the A P five treated birds could still tell finch song from canary song, so they

46
00:03:01.043 --> 00:03:02.283
were not simply deaf to it.

47
00:03:03.217 --> 00:03:07.217
So NMDA receptors there are needed while the song is memorized.

48
00:03:07.917 --> 00:03:09.697
Where the memory is stored is not shown.
